 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nLOCK statement parsing broken for certain keywords\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen parsing SQL LOCK statements, certain keywords like ROW, IN, and READ are not being properly handled, causing the parser to fail or produce incorrect results.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport sqlglot\n\n# This should parse correctly but doesn't\nsql = \"LOCK TABLE my_table IN ROW SHARE MODE\"\nparsed = sqlglot.parse_one(sql)\nprint(parsed)\n\n# Another example that fails\nsql2 = \"LOCK TABLE users FOR READ\"  \nparsed2 = sqlglot.parse_one(sql2)\nprint(parsed2)\n```\n\nThe parser seems to skip over these keywords instead of properly assigning them to the lock statement components.\n\n#### Expected Behavior\n\nThe LOCK statement should correctly parse and preserve all the lock-related keywords like ROW, IN, and READ in the appropriate fields of the parsed expression.\n\n#### Actual Behavior\n\nThe parser appears to ignore certain keywords during LOCK statement parsing, resulting in incomplete or incorrect parse trees.\n</issue>\n",
